## 4. Software Architecture (Tuya / Matter)

The adapter is **always-on** (external PSU + permanent Wi-Fi). Scheduling, time windows, and notifications are handled in **Tuya Smart Life automations** — not on-device.

### 4.1. On-Device Logic

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| Ring detected | GPIO4 goes LOW |
| Duration filter | Accept only 1.0 – 3.0 s (reject noise & stuck buttons) |
| Valid ring | Report **Contact Sensor** event to Matter → Tuya |
| Door open | **OnOff switch** endpoint; Tuya automation triggers it → 2 s PhotoMOS pulse |

### 4.2. Tuya Automation Example

```
WHEN  Intercom Ring sensor → Open
THEN  Intercom Door switch  → ON
      (optional) Send phone notification
      (optional) Time condition: 16:00 – 20:00
```

All schedule logic lives in the app. No NTP, no RTC, no offline clock required.

### 4.3. OLED Display States

| State | Screen |
|-------|--------|
| Boot / idle | `READY` + `TUYA OK` + `WiFi OK` |
| Commissioning | `PAIRING...` / `Smart Life` |
| Ring active | `>>> RING <<<` + duration timer |
| Event sent | `EVENT SENT` → `Tuya` |
| Door opening | `DOOR OPEN` + progress bar |
| No network | `NO WiFi` |

---

## 5. Power & Connectivity Constraints

**Power stealing is forbidden.** The ESP32-C3 draws up to 350 mA during Wi-Fi TX — far beyond what the intercom line can supply (~1–2 mA). Use an external 5 V PSU (mini AC/DC module in the junction box).

**Always-on mode.** Deep sleep is not used: Wi-Fi must stay connected for instant Matter event delivery to Tuya.

---

## 6. Flashing

1. Open [`intercom-adapter.ino`](intercom-adapter.ino) in Arduino IDE.
2. Board: **ESP32C3 Dev Module**.
3. Install libraries: **Arduino Matter**, **U8g2**.
4. Flash via USB (disconnect from intercom wiring first).
5. Commission in **Smart Life** app (Matter pairing).
6. Create Tuya automation: ring sensor → door switch.
